---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import audit from "../data/audit-negatives.json";
const wrong = audit.filter((r) => r.outcome === "WRONG");
const standing = audit.filter((r) => r.outcome === "STANDING");

/* This page used to say «five of nine» in its own prose, in four places, while
   the table beside it was drawn from the data. On 20 September a sixth and a
   seventh negative were proven wrong and the sentences went on saying five. So
   the counts are written out from the data now, and the page cannot drift from
   its own table again. */
const WORD = ["no", "one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven",
              "eight", "nine", "ten", "eleven", "twelve", "thirteen"];
const word = (n) => WORD[n] || String(n);
const Word = (n) => { const w = word(n); return w[0].toUpperCase() + w.slice(1); };
---
<Base title="The Audit" eyebrow="Every negative in this archive, and what it rests on"
      dek={`Twice in one week a search that had been closed turned out to be open. So each negative was re-read — not to repeat it, but to ask what set its window. ${Word(wrong.length)} of ${word(audit.length)} had already failed.`}>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{audit.length}</div><div class="l">negatives audited</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{wrong.length}</div><div class="l">already proven wrong</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{standing.length}</div><div class="l">still standing, same shape</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>A negative in this archive is not a shrug. It is a claim: <em>the record is not in these
      volumes</em>. Each one here was read properly — complete alphabetical sections, bounded above
      and below, written up with what was in them.</p>
      <p><strong>And {word(wrong.length)} of the {word(audit.length)} were wrong anyway.</strong> Not because the reading failed, but
      because <strong>the window was set by something soft</strong> — a declared age, or an assumption
      about which child came first — and the search then honoured that window exactly.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{Word(wrong.length)} that failed</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Where the window came from, and how far out it was</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What was sought</th><th class="m">Window</th><th>Set by</th>
          <th class="m">Out by</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {wrong.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{r.who}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{r.window}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.setBy}</td>
              <td class="m"><strong>{r.missBy}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The rule that falls out of it</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Every failed window was derived from one of two things, and both are soft:</p>
      <p style="margin-left:1.2rem"><strong>A declared age.</strong> Giovanni Arena's was three years
      out. Serafina Arlotta's was three years out. Michele Donato's tree date was four years out. In
      this archive ages are wrong by <strong>one to four years as a matter of routine</strong>, in
      either direction.</p>
      <p style="margin-left:1.2rem"><strong>An assumed eldest child.</strong> Francesco Antonio
      Arena's marriage was hunted in 1858–59 because his "first" son was born in 1860. He married in
      <strong>1851</strong>, and three elder children were simply unfound at the time.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>So: a window derived from a declared age or an
      assumed first child must be widened by at least five years in both directions before a negative
      inside it means anything.</strong> Four consecutive years were read for Giovanni Arena and the
      answer sat four years outside them. Three were read for Michele Donato and the answer sat one
      year outside them. <strong>A tested negative is worth more than an untested positive — but only
      if what was tested was the right window.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{Word(standing.length)} that still stand</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">And are the same shape</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What is sought</th><th class="m">Window</th><th>Set by</th><th>Why it is fragile</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {standing.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{r.who}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{r.window}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.setBy}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>The weakest is Giovanni Arlotta × Maria
    Costa</strong> — a single year, 1829, excluded on the assumption that Serafina was their first
    child. He was born about 1779. A marriage anywhere in the civil window would make him thirty-seven
    to fifty, which reads as a <em>second</em> marriage with the first pre-civil and parish-only. The
    negative is true and proves almost nothing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What was also caught</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><strong>And one of a different kind again, on 20 September: a negative that hardened into a
      published wall.</strong> Filippo Arena's marriage was hunted through 1816, 1817, 1818 and 1819.
      All four years were read properly and all four negatives were correct. The conclusion drawn from
      them — <em>the marriage is pre-civil, the Arena line stops here</em> — was written up as one of
      this archive's four established walls and stood on the site for five days. <strong>Scilla's
      registers begin in 1809.</strong> They married on <strong>12 November 1815</strong>, and the act
      names his parents and hers. The wall has been removed. <strong>Every other window on this page
      was set by something soft; this one was set by a fact nobody checked</strong>, which is the
      worse failure, because a soft window invites doubt and a wrong fact ends it.</p>

      <p>Two errors of a different kind turned up while this page was being written, and both are on
      the pages where they belong.</p>
      <p><strong>An identification made on a name and a year.</strong> A FindMyPast record —
      <em>Clementina Arena, Ellis Island, 1921</em> — had sat in this archive for months and had never
      been opened. The line above hers on the same manifest names a father and a residence four
      hundred kilometres away. See <a href={u("/crossings")}>The Crossings</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>A year that looked read but wasn't.</strong> Two arks in this archive's own list of
      Scilla marriage volumes lead to <em>notificazioni</em> books rather than the marriage acts. The
      list does not distinguish volume types, so a year can be marked read when the wrong book was
      opened. See <a href={u("/scilla")}>Scilla</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
